{"type":"click","text":"C\n    \n      Hybrid: Cliff + South Face Overlay\n      Use the cliff system for elevation, z-order, lighting, side edges, and corners. THEN overlay the south-facing wall face sprite ON TOP of the cliff face for visual richness (brick texture, windows, doors). Best of both worlds.\n      \n        How it works:\n        - Floor at elevation +1 in chunk pipeline (cliff system kicks in)\n        - Cliff renders: south face, side edges, corners — correct lighting\n        - South face: wall sprite overlaid on cliff face (brick + windows)\n        - East/West: cliff edge handles it (thin dark line, correct)\n        - Corners: cliff corner tiles (already seamless)\n        Cliff does structure. Sprite does beauty.\n      \n      \n        Pros\n          Correct z-order from cliff system\n          Correct lighting from cliff system\n          Corners and side edges solved by cliffs\n          Rich south face from dedicated sprite (windows/doors)\n          Existing wall face sprites reused (256px brick + variants)\n        \n        Cons\n          Two systems (cliff + overlay) = more complexity\n          South face sprite must align with cliff face height\n          Need to figure out how to inject elevation into chunk compiler for buildings","choice":"c","id":null,"timestamp":1781392310288}
{"type":"click","text":"B\n    \n      Sprite Wall Segments\n      Each wall direction is a separate sprite type. South = tall face sprite (256px). East/West = dedicated side-view sprites generated at correct angle. North = thin cap sprite. Corners = dedicated corner sprites. All drawn as overlay objects after terrain.\n      \n        How it works:\n        - South: wall face sprite (what we have now, just tiled correctly)\n        - East/West: new side-angle wall sprite (PixelLab, slight perspective)\n        - North: thin cap or invisible (facing away)\n        - Corners: dedicated corner piece sprites\n        - Each type generated separately via PixelLab\n        Needs: 4+ sprite types per wall material, composited manually\n      \n      \n        Pros\n          Full control over each wall direction's look\n          Can make walls any height (not limited by cliff system)\n          Each direction is a custom sprite — maximum visual quality\n        \n        Cons\n          4+ sprite types per material = lots of PixelLab generation\n          Manual z-ordering (current broken approach)\n          Lighting mismatch (sprites bypass chunk pipeline)\n          Corner seams hard to get right without Wang system\n          Side wall perspective hard to get right in top-down","choice":"b","id":null,"timestamp":1781392321040}
